## Part C: Novelty and literature

### C1. The reliability law has deep psychometric roots
- The reliability of difference scores is a classic topic: Lord (1956, 1963); Cronbach & Furby (1970); Rogosa & Willett (1983); Zimmerman & Williams (1982, 1998).
- Theorem 3.2 is an equivalent but cleaner parameterisation via ηS/ηN. That is a genuine contribution, since it explains in one ratio why difference scores can be more or less reliable than their parts, but it must be presented as such, with those citations.
- R²_D = Rel(D)·q is classical attenuation by measurement error; cite e.g. Fuller, *Measurement Error Models* (1987).

### C2. Other connections to cite
- **(z, r²) reduction:** the liability-threshold model (Falconer 1965). Liability-to-observed-scale conversions and AUC: Lee et al. (2012); Wray et al. (2010). Also the binormal ROC model.
- **η = 1/(1 − u)** with u = 2σ_AB/(σ²_A + σ²_B), the pairwise ICC (Lin's concordance coefficient with no location shift). Add as **P6**, citing Lin (1989). This links to geometry review B1.
- **Alignment as a restricted regression:** cite a standard regression text so referees don't think it is claimed as new.

### C3. Where the novelty lies
A synthesis that separates three quantities practitioners routinely conflate:
1. efficiency (η);
2. alignment (whether D suffices);
3. reliability (ηS/ηN).

Plus a quantified cost of ignoring alignment (1/(2n), crossover) and a map of which conclusions survive non-Gaussian data. This framing is honest and does not depend on any single result being new.

